questions.background:
Since the start of [hostilities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war) on February 28, 2026, US and Iranian negotiators have been publicly reported to have held four in-person rounds of negotiations:&#x20;

April 11-12, 2026 at the Serena Hotel in Islamabad, Pakistan -- There were 21 hours of negotiations between JD Vance, Steve Witkoff, Jared Kushner on the US side and Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf and Abbas Araghchi on the Iranian side, with Pakistan serving as the mediator

June 21–28, 2026 at the Bürgenstock Resort at Lake Lucerne, Switzerland -- There were two rounds of negotiations, with Vance, Witkoff, and Kushner on the US side and Ghalibaf and Araghchi on the Iranian side, with Pakistan and Qatar serving as mediators.

July 1-2, 2026 in Doha, Qatar -- There were talks between Witkoff and Kushner on the US side and Kazem Gharibabadi on the Iranian side, with Qatar serving as mediator.&#x20;

September 22, 2026 in New York, NY on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assemply -- There were talks between Witkoff and Kushner on the US side and Araghchi and Iran's UN delegation on the Iranian side.  Reportedly the meeting was not face-to-face but instead had Qatari Prime Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman Al Thani shuttling between the teams.

The [Strait of Hormuz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strait_of_Hormuz) is a narrow waterway connecting the Persian Gulf to the ocean, via the Gulf of Oman. Due to the amount of oil production in the Persian Gulf, 25% of the world's seaborne oil trade, as well as 20% of the world's liquefied natural gas, passes through the strait.&#x20;

On February 28, 2026, Israel and the United States began [a sustained campaign of airstrikes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war) against Iran. The strikes began a regional war, with Iran retaliating against Israel and the United States, as well as their assets in countries throughout the region. Shortly after the war began, Iran declared that ships would not be allowed to transit the Strait, and on March 1, 2026, Iran attacked several oil tankers, which kicked off the [2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Strait_of_Hormuz_crisis).&#x20;

Please find a spreadsheet with the data [here](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1T4hQ_Z3DOQ3OUZ_8V-HEsM7acqa-lZf4-2tl_sfWLyU/edit?gid=0#gid=0). Before the conflict, an average of 83 ships transited the Strait each day during the period of January 1, 2019 through February 28, 2026. Traffic did fluctuate, and the lowest recent 7-day moving average was 48 in December 2025, which for purposes of this question will be considered the minimum threshold for "normal" commercial traffic.&#x20;

In April 2026 the US and Iran agreed to a [ceasefire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war_ceasefire) and then in June 2026 a 60-day memorandum of understanding (MOU), resulting in a partial reopening of the Strait. On June 24, 2026, following the signing of the MOU, traffic reached 51 ships.

However, a low-level conflict continued through the summer, as the MOU broke down and the Strait remained closed. In the 7 days through September 20, 2026, an average of only 3 ships per day transited the waterway.

On February 28, 2026 the US in coordination with Israel [launched](https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48887) airstrikes on Iran, sparking a serious regional conflict until a two-week [ceasefire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war_ceasefire) on April 8, 2026 (with additional low-level conflicts and blockades ongoing as of September 2026). Soon after the strikes began, Iran announced the [closure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Strait_of_Hormuz_crisis) of the Strait of Hormuz, where normally 25% of the world's seaborne crude oil and 20% of its liquid natural gas transits.  In June 2026 the US and Iran agreed to a 60-day memorandum of understanding, resulting in a partial reopening of the Strait. However, a low-level conflict continued through the summer, as the MOU broke down and the Strait remained closed.

There have been repeated reports ([example](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/15/could-the-us-take-control-of-irans-southern-islands)) that one scenario being contemplated in Washington is for the US to occupy southern Iranian islands in order to enforce the free flow of oil and natural gas through the Strait. Another scenario [contemplated](https://www.wjcl.com/article/us-military-secret-mission-capture-iran-uranium-trump-paused-it-sources-iran-war-invade/71573017) has been a ground operation to send US troops into Iran to seize uranium. US president Donald Trump is [reported](https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/trump-rejects-iran-ceasefire-expects-renewed-bombing-after-midterms-5982ee50) to have told aides he planned to resume bombing Iran after the US midterms on November 3, 2026.&#x20;

In early April 2026, a special forces [operation](https://www.militarytimes.com/news/your-military/2026/04/08/the-rescue-mission-that-brought-2-f-15e-strike-eagle-crew-members-home/) involving hundreds of personnel were deployed into Iran to rescue a downed pilot, although the operation was reported to have lasted a number of hours rather than consecutive days.&#x20;

John Mearsheimer, author of *The Great Delusion: Liberal Dreams and International Realities*, has [speculated](https://mearsheimer.substack.com/p/will-trump-go-kamikaze) that a possible scenario could be seizing a small Iranian island, though due to small numbers of US troop numbers in the Middle East, it might be hard to hold the territory. In a September 2025 video [interview](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y2q4succ04o) Mearsheimer discusses the challenges faced by the US in its military strategy with Iran.&#x20;

Realist Stephen Walt, author of *The Hell of Good Intentions: America's Foreign Policy Elite and the Decline of U.S. Primacy*, in August 2026 [discussed](https://www.aljazeera.com/video/the-bottom-line/2026/8/23/trump-has-run-out-of-options-in-iran-war-stephen-walt) in an August 2026 interview his view that the strategic situation may be at a stalemate, due to Iran "caring more" about the outcome cancelling out the superiority of America's military strength, similar historically to conflicts such as Vietnam.

Francis Fukuyama recently on Bloomberg said that air strikes alone would not be sufficient to bring about the US's objective of regime change in Iran, though he also expressed skepticism that the US would put boots on the ground.

questions.market_info_resolution_criteria:
This question resolves **YES** if, between 1 January 2026 and 31 December 2026 (UTC), any of the following entities publishes a Sudan-war total **excess-mortality estimate (direct + indirect)** with a **central / point estimate exceeding 200,000 deaths**, applied at **nationwide** scope (i.e., covering "Sudan", "the Sudan war", or "the conflict" - not a single state, single city, or single event):

1. A peer-reviewed article in a PubMed-indexed medical or epidemiological journal (the *Lancet* family, *BMJ* family, *NEJM*, *JAMA*, *PLOS*, *Nature*, *Science*, *Conflict and Health*, or similar);
2. A working paper or preprint by a named academic research group with a university affiliation, hosted on SSRN, medRxiv, ResearchGate, or an institutional site (e.g., LSHTM Sudan Research Group, Yale Humanitarian Research Lab, UCLouvain CRED);
3. A formal publication of a UN body (UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission for Sudan, OHCHR, OCHA, WHO, UNICEF) presenting the figure as the body's own estimate, not a citation of others;
4. A formal report or press release with stated methodology from a major international NGO with epidemiological capacity (MSF, ICRC, IRC, Save the Children, or similar);
5. A national government report (US State Department, UK FCDO, EU EUAA, or similar) presenting its own modelled figure (not a citation);
6. A documented release or revision of the [CRED EM-DAT](https://www.emdat.be/) or [Uppsala UCDP](https://ucdp.uu.se/) database that lists a Sudan-war cumulative total exceeding the threshold, accompanied by a public report or release note.

This question resolves **NO** if no such publication appears by 31 December 2026 (UTC).

questions.background:
According to the [Wall Street Journal](https://www.wsj.com/world/russia/ten-months-of-war-to-gain-46-square-miles-how-russia-is-failing-on-the-battlefield-3184ae10) on November 6, 2026:&#x20;

> With its encirclement plan thwarted, Russia is now pouring even more infantry into one last attempt to break through Ukrainian lines this year. Its target is the town of Dobropillia, southwest of Kramatorsk.

> Russia is using the same tactics that it has used across eastern Ukraine since 2025: Again and again, tiny groups of infantry try to sneak past Ukrainian lines on foot, while Ukrainian drones hunt them.

> Russian troops outnumber Ukrainians by about 6-to-1 in the area, said Kyrylo Dubrovskyi, a lieutenant colonel with the First Corps of Ukraine’s National Guard. In addition, he said, Moscow has deployed about a third of its elite Rubicon drone force to the sector—a sign of Dobropillia’s importance for the Kremlin now.

> A drone pilot from First Corps said the Russians were making some territorial gains, but at a huge price. The corps posted a video showing dozens of Russian corpses on a road outside Dobropillia.

Dobropillia, a mining town located in northwest Donetsk with a pre-war population of 28,000, has been near frontline combat operations in the Russian-Ukraine War [since](https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2025/08/13/russian-army-says-it-captured-2-villages-near-dobropillia-in-eastern-ukraine-a90197) August 2025. By the summer of 2026, it had become completely [cut off](https://frontliner.ua/en/line-of-contact-nears-dobropillia-city-isolated/) from other parts of Ukraine via road vehicle transport.&#x20;

According to ISW in its November 1, 2026 [update](https://understandingwar.org/research/russia-ukraine/russian-offensive-campaign-assessment-november-1-2026/):

> Russian forces have recently made limited gains toward Dobropillya, primarily in the form of small-group infiltration missions, but have not consolidated any positions in the town.

As of November 7, 2026, the most recently updated ISW map had assessed Russian advances less than 3 miles (5 kilometers) from the building:&#x20;

![](https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/Screenshot_2026-11-07_at_12.19.18PM.png)

On December 15, 2025, fintech [pioneer](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/paypal-mafia-built-1-5-175102356.html) PayPal, which also owns Venmo, [announced](https://investor.pypl.com/news-and-events/news-details/2025/PayPal-Submits-Applications-to-Establish-an-Industrial-Bank-to-Expand-Access-to-Financial-Services-for-U-S--Small-Businesses/default.aspx) that it had submitted applications to regulators to establish PayPal Bank in the United States. Technically its aim is to form an Industrial Loan Company (ILC), which [is able to](https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R46489) operate in many ways like a bank (by taking FDIC-insured deposits and making loans) while at the same time having exemptions from the Bank Holding Company Act of 1956.&#x20;

PayPal's application sparked strong opposition from groups such as the [Independent Community Bankers of America](https://www.independentbanker.org/w/icba-others-oppose-paypal-s-ilc-application) and the [Bank Policy Institute](https://bpi.com/bpi-and-icba-comment-on-paypal-banks-application-for-deposit-insurance/), who argued that PayPal Bank's parent company's exemption from the BHCA could give it unfair competitive advantages relative to traditional banks and result in excess loans, creating risks to the Deposit Insurance Fund.&#x20;

FDIC Chairman Travis Hill has [called for](https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2025/statement-acting-chairman-travis-hill) more de novo banks "so there is a healthy pipeline of new entrants in the banking sector." Recently, the FDIC [approved](https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/crypto/articles/ford-gm-stellantis-join-crypto-172558720.html) applications for automakers Ford, GM and Stellantis to form banks and is [considering](https://www.bankingdive.com/news/upstart-apply-occ-fdic-fed-national-bank-charter-ai-lending/814429/) an application from AI-lending marketplace fintech [Upstart](https://www.upstart.com/).&#x20;

Historically, applications to the FDIC by companies that are not traditional banks have faced an uphill battle. [Walmart](https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna17645331) and [Home Depot](https://hbsdealer.com/news/home-depot-rescinds-bid-enerbank) have had to give up on their efforts to either start or acquire a bank. SoFi, Nelnet and Square eventually received approvals, but only after undergoing years of scrutiny and regulatory battles.&#x20;

Loudoun County, Virginia, sits at the center of the largest concentration of data-center infrastructure in the United States and has experienced exceptionally rapid growth in recent years.&#x20;

The county has historically been highly receptive to new development. From 2022 through May 2026, the Loudoun County Board of Supervisors approved 32 of 39 legislative applications for data centers that came before it.&#x20;

Another 21 applications representing approximately 56 data-center buildings and 20.7 million square feet were still moving through the county’s legislative process as of the county’s latest published overview. In Fiscal Year 2026, the sector generated approximately \$1.2 billion in real and personal property-tax revenue, equal to 39% of the county’s overall budget; the FY2027 budget projects approximately \$1.3 billion, or 40% of the total budget.&#x20;

However on September 15, 2026, a majority of the Board voted to consider a resolution that could pause the Board’s final action on legislative applications for data centers and electrical substations for up to 12 months. The proposal followed a county-attorney opinion that an outright moratorium on data-center approvals would not be legal under Virginia law.

The proposed pause is intended to give Loudoun time to complete a broader review of its Comprehensive Plan and Zoning Ordinance rules for data centers, a process currently expected to continue into summer 2027. Applications could continue to be filed and move through the Planning Commission during the pause, but final Board votes would generally be deferred.

The Board initially scheduled the resolution for October 20 but formally deferred consideration to November 17, 2026, stating that staff needed additional information before a final decision.

Democrats [won control of both the House and Senate](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/democrats-capture-full-control-congress-nbc-news-projects-fueled-victo-rcna607042) on November 3, 2026, with [at least 54 Senate seats](https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/election-day-2026-midterms/). The outgoing Republican Congress returns for a lame-duck session facing a [December 11 expiration of the current continuing resolution](https://www.crfb.org/blogs/appropriations-watch-fy-2027). No full-year FY2027 appropriations bills have been enacted, and the House has passed only three of the twelve.&#x20;

Trump's second term has already seen three funding lapses: A 43-day shutdown began October 1, 2025; a four-day partial shutdown followed from January 31 to February 3, 2026; and a shutdown of the Department of Homeland Security alone then ran from February 14 to April 30, 2026, and at 76 days became the [longest shutdown of a single federal department in US history](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/dhs-shutdown-house-vote/).&#x20;

Senate Democrats have [resisted the administration's request](https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-live-senate-democrats-block-1-trillion-annual-defense-bill-in-protest-over-iran-war) to raise defense spending to \$1.5 trillion, up from about \$900 billion. Appropriators in both parties [have said](https://thehill.com/homenews/house/6169401-midterm-elections-government-funding-fight-shutdown/) they expect another short-term extension if the bills are not finished, and that the odds of a shutdown are low. House Appropriations ranking member Rosa DeLauro said Democrats want full-year funding done before the new Congress convenes on January 3, 2027, rather than inherit it. Before the election, Trump said that under a Democratic Congress ["I veto everything, and then the Democrats come and they want to make a deal"](https://rollcall.com/2026/10/26/trumps-veto-everything-threat-puts-funding-debt-limit-in-spotlight/).

questions.market_info_resolution_criteria:
This market will resolve to "Yes" if a state of ceasefire remains in effect between Israel and Iran through the listed date, 11:59 PM Iran Standard Time (IRST). Otherwise this market will resolve to “No”. 

A state of ceasefire will be considered to no longer be in effect between Israel and Iran if either Israel or Iran takes a qualifying military action against the other country between market creation and the listed date, 11:59 PM IRST.

A qualifying military action refers to an air strike or a surface-to-surface missile strike, initiated by Israel or Iran, that directly impacts the other country. An air strike includes bombs, air-to-surface missiles, and air-launched drones. A surface-to-surface missile strike includes one-way attack drones and surface-to-surface missiles such as cruise or ballistic missiles.

The following actions do not constitute a qualifying military action:
Munitions destroyed or intercepted before impact;
Surface-to-air missile strikes;
Small-arms fire;
Ground incursions;
Cyber operations;
Naval gunfire and artillery fire;
Howitzers, artillery pieces, mortars, and rocket artillery (e.g. MLRS systems);
Minor surface-to-surface strikes, including short range loitering munitions, FPV drones, and ATGM strikes;
Any threat, authorization, or announcement of force that has not been executed.

Any munition that is intercepted or destroyed before impact does not constitute a qualifying military action. Debris, fragments, or any wreckage from intercepted munitions that land on Israel or Iran do not constitute a military action regardless of any damage incurred.

A military action will be considered to impact Israel or Iran if it directly impacts the terrestrial territory of Israel or Iran, including any internal waters. Maritime territory and airspace are not encompassed. Where territorial borders are disputed, all territory claimed by and under the de facto control of the relevant state as of market creation will qualify.

The occurrence, attribution, and timing of a qualifying military action will be primarily determined based on a consensus of information available from the resolution sources. Where multiple sources conflict regarding the occurrence, attribution, or timing of a relevant military action, this market will remain open until the earlier of: i) the confirmation of the occurrence, attribution, and timing of the action based on a consensus of information available from the resolution sources or ii) 3 full calendar days (IRST) from the date of the first credibly reported evidence of the action. If this period would extend past the end date, this market will remain open to allow for 3 full calendar days to pass. If, at the end of the third calendar day, conflicting reports remain as to the occurrence, attribution, or timing of the action, this market will resolve based on the totality of information available from the resolution sources at that time. No single statement, denial, or presentation of evidence will govern where it is contradicted by the totality of information.

The resolution sources for this market will be official information from the governments and militaries of Israel and Iran and credible reporting.

